"Organizes evidence about what a person says, thinks, does and feels in a specific situation.\n\nUse when: Build a shared view of research observations before design decisions. Expose gaps between reported intentions and observed behavior.\n\nAvoid when: The team lacks user evidence and would present speculation as established knowledge.\n\nVisual grammar: Four labeled regions distinguish says, thinks, does and feels. A central actor and context anchor all observations. Evidence tags separate direct statements, observations and interpreted mental states.\n\nBuild: Choose one participant or a clearly defined research-based segment. Sort interview and observation evidence into the four regions. Identify contradictions, knowledge gaps and questions for further research.\n\nCheck: Direct quotations and interpretations are distinguished. All observations share a stated context and actor. Conflicting evidence is retained rather than averaged away.\n\nPitfalls: Inventing private thoughts from a demographic stereotype. Mixing many unrelated users and situations in one map.\n\nExample: A commuter explains a ticket purchase, hesitates at fare choices and reports uncertainty, helping distinguish observed actions from inferred concerns.\n\nAlternatives: Persona profile, Customer journey map."
